This would be because there isn't one.
In Greek mythology there's gods "all the way down", so to speak; in Norse
mythology the basic structure of the universe, as well as its origin, is
a given; it wasn't caused by anyone/anything. This obviously includes
time.

A better interpretation of the Norns is that they are the cause of
*structured* time, which fits their role as fates pretty well - without
them there would be none.

That is, time is anyway, but the Norns give it structure by weaving
everything's fate into it, and enforcing said fate. They are the cause of
beginnings and ends, so if you want to be extreme you could say that they
are the cause of linear (iterative) time. (As opposed to recursive time.
There are some good simulations on this theme.)


